  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CONSERVATION OF CHIHUAHUAN DESERT SHORT</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Grassland Priority Conservation Area El Tokio hosts huge biodiversity, some of which is endemic. Nevertheless, it lacks sufficient protection and conservation actions are scattered. El Tokio hosts the Sprague&apos;s Pipit and Mountain Plover, two grassland priority species that both need short grasslands with a certain percentage of naked soil for their reproductive and winter populations. These habitats are highly threatened with only 20% of the short grasslands remaining in the Mexican Plateau, and nearly 50% of it is in poor condition. The most important threats are land-use change, overgrazing, and encroachment, linked with low incomes and lack of knowledge from the communities. This second phase will protect 15,893 acres of key grasslands, tackle desertification in 301 acres, deliver ranching regenerative management in 6,689 acres, and improve access to information by using extension specialists to increase the engagement of landowners to conserve grasslands not only for birds as Sprague&apos;s Pipit and Mountain Plover but also for them and their livelihoods.</narrative>
  </description>
